## rooms.add
**Room:** a random rectangle that overlaps or touches no room placed so far, carved as a room [[c01:22-46]]. Room {n}.

## rooms.discard
**Discarded:** this rectangle overlaps or touches a room already placed [[c01:22-53 t:54-55]]. Rooms so far: {n}.

## rooms.sort
**Sort:** the rooms, by their left edge: the order they are joined in [[c01:57-69]]. Rooms to join: {n}.

## rooms.corridor
**Corridor:** from this room’s center to the center of the room before it. One run across and one up or down; a coin flip picks which comes first [[c01:67-78 c01:85-101 t:57-58]]. Corridor {n}.

## bsp.subdivide
**The list:** it starts with one rectangle inside the map’s edge, and that rectangle’s four quarters [[c02:24-29 c02:87-111]]. Quarters shown: {n}.

## bsp.add
**Attempt:** pick any rectangle on the list and try a room near its top-left corner [[c02:43-44 c02:114-136]]. The room is carved only if every tile within 2 of it is still solid and inside the map’s outer ring. Then the rectangle it came from is quartered again [[c02:45-52 c02:86-112 c02:138-174]]. Room {n}.

## cellular.noise
**Noise:** every tile, the map’s edge included, starts as wall with a {n}% chance, else as floor [[c03:19-29]].

## cellular.iterate
**A pass:** every tile inside the map’s outer ring counts the walls among its 8 neighbors, in a copy of the map made before the pass [[c03:40-70 t:79-80]]. 5 or more, or none: wall. 1 to 4: floor [[c03:61-66 t:81-82]]. Pass {n}.

## drunkard.solid
**Start:** a solid map [[c04:17-19 t:87]].

## drunkard.dig
**Digger:** steps in a random direction and digs the tile it lands on, again and again, until it steps off the map or runs out of steps [[c04:54-76 t:90-95]]. The first starts at the center; each later one on a random open tile [[c04:21-22 c04:25-47 t:95-96]]. Digger {n}.

## dla.seed
**Seed:** a plus of five open tiles at the center of a solid map: the open floor the first particles have to reach [[c05:19-40 t:103-105]].

## dla.particle
**Walk:** from a random tile, one random step at a time, until it steps onto open floor. Digs the solid tile it stepped from [[c05:49-82 t:104-108]]. Particle {n}.

## dla.particle#c06-dla-attractor
**Attractor:** from a random tile, straight toward the center. Digs the last solid tile it stood on before open floor [[c06:49-68 t:111-112]]. Particle {n}.

## dla.particle#c07-dla-symmetry
**Symmetric:** straight toward the center, as the attractor. The tile it digs is dug again at its mirror image across the middle column [[c07:49-77 t:115-116]]. Particle {n}.

## dla-erode.start
**Start:** a finished map. In the talk’s demo: random rooms joined by corridors [[c08:21-83 t:191-192]].

## dla-erode.particle
**Particle:** starts on a random open tile and steps at random until it stands on rock. That one tile of rock is dug [[c08:86-128 t:192-193]]. Particle {n}.

## voronoi.seeds
**Scatter:** seed points, at random, inside the map’s outer ring [[c08a:21-26 t:121-122]]. Seeds: {n}.

## voronoi.membership.pythagoras
**Cells, by straight-line distance:** every tile joins its nearest seed. The edges come out fairly smooth [[c08a:36-52 t:125-126 t:130]].

## voronoi.membership.manhattan
**Cells, by Manhattan distance:** the blocks across plus the blocks down. The edges turn sharp [[c08a:57-73 t:131-133]].

## voronoi.membership.chebyshev
**Cells, by Chebyshev distance:** the talk places the result somewhere between the other two [[c08a:77-93 t:134-135]].

## voronoi.walls
**Walls:** a tile inside the map’s outer ring becomes a wall when the tile to its right or the tile below it belongs to another seed [[c08a:96-113 t:137-138]]. Seeds: {n}.

## voronoi.roads
**Roads, our own rule,** for a use the talk describes without code. The edges become road and the cells solid. A tile is road when its right, lower or lower-right neighbor belongs to another seed [[own]]. Seeds: {n}.

## noise.map
**Height map:** fractal simplex noise, sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 [[c10:11-26]].

## noise.map#c11-overworld
**Overworld:** noise, sampled once at every tile and read as altitude. Below 0: water. From 0 up to 0.5: grass. 0.5 or more: mountain [[c11:20-44]].

## noise.first
**First noise,** on its own, with the demo’s base settings [[c13:23-29 c13:71-105]].

## noise.second
**Second noise,** on its own: another seed, half the octaves and gain, 1 more of lacunarity, 4 times the frequency [[c13:31-37 c13:108-135]]. Talk says: much more bumpy, and terrible as a continent [[t:169-171]].

## noise.zoomed-out
**Zoomed out:** the noise at scale 1, cut into 3 bands by its value: the talk’s water, green terrain and mountains [[c12:31-47 c12:66-76 t:164-166]].

## noise.zoomed-out#c13-double-noise
**Zoomed out:** the two noises mixed at scale 1. The first counts in full; the second is taken away at a quarter of its strength [[c13:39-44 c13:139-149]].

## noise.scale
**Zoom:** every tile’s sample position is multiplied by a scale that shrinks a hundredth at a time, so a smaller and smaller part of the same noise fills the map [[c12:31-33 c12:77-91 t:167]]. Scale, in hundredths: {n}.

## noise.scale#c13-double-noise
**Zoom, two noises:** as the scale shrinks, the first noise’s weight falls with it to a half and the second’s rises to a quarter. Both stay there from a scale of 0.5 down [[c13:39-44 c13:150-164 t:171-173]]. Scale, in hundredths: {n}.

## combine.input
**An input:** built on a map of its own. In the talk’s demo: rooms in subdivided space, then a cellular automata map [[c09:41-79 c09:82-97 t:181-182]]. Input {n}.

## combine.halves
**Halves:** the left half of the map is copied from the first input and the right half from the second, column for column. Nothing joins them [[c09:100-114 t:183-185]].

## prefab.show
**The prefab,** on its own: 2 treasure tiles ringed by traps, inside a margin that is left as the map has it [[c14:86-102 c14:149-155 t:200-201]].

## prefab.placed
**Placed** where it fits: at the center of a room big enough for it, or on a patch of open floor [[c14:104-124 c15:41-67 t:203-206]].

## prefab.placed#c09-combination
**Placed** down the middle of the map, over the seam between the two halves [[c09:116-133 t:186-188]]. The talk’s: a line of fortifications. Ours: a hall of our own design, because we don’t copy its layout [[own]].

## prefab.no-place
**No place found** for the prefab [[own]]. The talk’s loops end only when the prefab has been placed, so its code would still be looking [[c14:104-124 c15:41-67]]. Ours stops and records the seed as a failure [[own]].

## cull.center
**Center:** the open tile nearest the center of the map is found [[c16:36-54 t:224]].

## cull.marked
**Marked:** a Dijkstra map from that tile gives every tile it can walk to a distance. Open tiles left without one can’t be reached [[c16:62-73 t:225-229]]. Tiles marked: {n}.

## cull.removed
**Removed:** open tiles that the open tile nearest the center can’t walk to are turned to wall [[c17:34-67 t:230]]. Tiles removed: {n}.

## start.desired
**Desired start:** in the talk’s demos, the middle of the left edge or the center of the map [[c17:70 c19:69 t:241-242]].

## start.placed
**Start:** the open tile nearest that point, in a straight line [[c17:71-86 t:242-243]].

## exit.placed
**Exit:** the open tile nearest the middle of the right edge. Then the route from the start to it is found [[c18:92-111 t:247-248]]. Steps on the shortest route: {n}.

## exit.placed#c19-ending-dijkstra
**Exit:** a Dijkstra map from the start gives every tile it can reach its walking distance. The exit goes on the tile with the largest: the least accessible one [[c19:92-103 t:249-250]]. Steps on the shortest route: {n}.

## exit.no-path
**No route:** the exit is placed, but nothing reaches it from the start. Without a cull, the two can land in separate caves [[own]].

## hot-path.marked
**Hot path:** a second Dijkstra map starts from every tile of the route at once, so each tile’s value is its distance from the route. Tiles under a set distance, 10 in the demo, are the hot path [[c20:131-145 t:260-262]]. Radius: {n}.

## hot-path.culled
**Cull:** tiles at the cull distance or farther from the route become wall. The talk’s demo culls at 15, wider than the 10 of its hot path [[c20:131-154 t:263-264]]. Cull distance: {n}.

## hot-rooms.marked
**Hot rooms:** each room is tested against every step of the route. The demo highlights the rooms that pass and grays the rest [[c21:129-156 t:267-269]]. Rooms on the route: {n}.
